      "id": 1,
      "text": "Valentine Eggar founded Pray in 1907, turning a ranching spot into a private town that remains owned by the same family.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pray%2C_Montana"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town takes its name from Congressman Charles Nelson Pray, who served Montana in the early 1900s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pray%2C_Montana"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Pray grew along a 1903 Northern Pacific Railway spur that linked Livingston to Yellowstone, making it a key stop for travelers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/pray_montana"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The 1862 Homestead Act opened Paradise Valley to settlers, sparking ranching and farming in the 1860s and 1870s, shaping Pray’s early growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/pray_montana"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Pray’s post office opened December 8, 1909, perched about 5,000 feet above sea level.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/pray_montana"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Nearby Chico Hot Springs offers geothermal waters, making Pray a gateway to Montana’s natural hot springs.",
